  analysis: "Ten-year Tier 1 review (2016–2025, all shifts): Reasoning is anchored by number/letter series (≈3), coding-decoding (≈3), analogy and classification (≈4 combined), syllogism (≈2), and non-verbal items—mirror/water, paper folding, embedded/figural series, dice—appearing in almost every year. Quant is led by data interpretation (≈5), mensuration and geometry (≈6–7 combined), trigonometry (≈2–3), and arithmetic (percentage, ratio, time-work, speed). English consistently mixes one reading passage (≈5), cloze (≈3–5), error spotting (≈2–3), voice/narration (≈3–4), and vocabulary. GA blends static polity/history/geography/science with 3–5 current-affairs items per shift; applied science questions have risen since 2024. These three papers mirror that mix; each has 100 unique PYQ-style items with no repeated stem inside the set.",
